<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/14th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · War</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kannō disturbance</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1350">1350</a>–<a href="/years/1352">1352</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a> · Also known as Kannō incident</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Kannō disturbance was a conflict of 1350 to 1352 within the Ashikaga <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shogun">shogunate</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>, between the shogun <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashikaga-takauji">Ashikaga Takauji</a> and his brother Tadayoshi, who for a time allied with the Southern Court. Southern forces exploited the split to seize <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyoto">Kyoto</a> in 1352 and carry off the retired Northern emperors Kōgon and Kōmyō and the deposed Sukō. Tadayoshi died in 1352, probably poisoned.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kanno-disturbance.jpg" alt="Kannō disturbance" width="320" height="399" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Fujiwara no Takanobu · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Minamoto_no_Yoritomo.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
